<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Ambracia</h1><p class="st-meta">625 BCE – 189 BCE (approx.) · capital Ambracia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Corinthian colony in north-west Greece that became the capital of King Pyrrhus and was besieged and plundered by Rome in 189 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ambracia was founded c. 625 BCE by Corinthians under Gorgus, a son of the tyrant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cypselus">Cypselus</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arachthos">Arachthos</a> river north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambracian-gulf">Ambracian Gulf</a>. It controlled fertile land and routes into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, and Cypselid relatives ruled it as tyrants until the early 6th century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ambracia was a loyal ally of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/corinth">Corinth</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sparta">Sparta</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> it fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acarnania">Acarnanians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amphilochian-argos">Amphilochian Argos</a>, and in 426 BCE the Athenian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demosthenes-general">Demosthenes</a> destroyed its army at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-idomene">Idomene</a>. It traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and goods with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrrhus-of-epirus">Pyrrhus</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-epirus">Kingdom of Epirus</a> made it his capital and adorned it with palaces and statues.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After joining the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a>, Ambracia was besieged by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-fulvius-nobilior">Marcus Fulvius Nobilior</a> in 189 BCE, who carried its works of art to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It declined, and Augustus moved its people to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicopolis-epirus">Nicopolis</a>. Modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arta-greece">Arta</a> stands on the site.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ambracia.jpg" alt="Ambracia" width="320" height="199"><figcaption>Image: Dodos2013 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Small_theatre_of_Ambracia_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>625 BCE – 189 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ambracia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Gorgus</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ambrakia, Arta</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-407&amp;focus=ambracia" aria-label="Ambracia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5199 774 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ambracia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5519 1030L5519 1030M5519 1030L5538 1049M5519 1030L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5519 1030h0M5538 1049h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5519 1030h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5519 1030h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-407&amp;focus=ambracia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

